# Pages that are deliberately over the 8 KB ceiling, and why.
#
# The ceiling exists because every byte of a page is paid by the agent that opens
# it. A page is normally split when it exceeds the ceiling. These two are not,
# because splitting them would cost the reader more than the bytes do: each is
# opened to look one thing up, and the answer would land on the other side of the
# split as often as not.
#
# An exception is not free. A page listed here is reported on every run, so the
# pressure to shrink it stays visible. An entry naming a page that does not exist,
# or one that is already under the ceiling, is a defect and is reported.
#
#   <page> = <bytes> # reason
#
22-state-machine.md = 18432  # the whole .fsml format, and it cannot be half-learned: History, OnFinal, Submachine, the guard grammar and the transition rules answer each other. The only other place any of them is written down is a 50 KB schema that gives the grammar and not the meaning, and a run that had to go there carried 13,000 tokens of it for sixty turns. Raised once more for the two facts a pause/resume design cannot be built without and neither the page nor the schema stated: that a restored state re-runs its EntryList, and that Source="Value" is a verbatim C++ token. Both were inferred from a recipe comment in a measured run; 685 bytes here is the cheapest they can be bought. Raised again for the queued OnFinal self-event: an operation on a nested Final runs before the composite is left, so the next request is refused as busy. Nothing in the page, the schema or the generated code said so, and finding it took 22 of 81 requests and 27% of one measured run. check_contract.py rule P-14 now reports the placement, but a rule that fires on a page that does not explain it only moves the cost
51-debug.md         = 13312  # a symptom index; a split makes the reader guess which half their symptom is in. "Running the pieces" already came out of it as 50-running.md. Raised once when the generator began printing rule numbers: the refusal section now shows the message shape it must be read against, which is what stops the reader guessing at a finding instead of quoting it
50-running.md       = 10240  # the page that turns "it built" into "it is proven to work". Raised when scenarios gained stdin, stop and reject: nearly every task asks a long-running multi-process application to prove a console quit path and a peer-loss path, and before those keys existed an agent hand-rolled mtrouter, sleep and pkill to check them -- 28 turns of one measured run. The console-loop shape here is the other half: a loop that treats end of input as a quit request shuts the service down in milliseconds with no error to read, and cost 55 turns of the next run. Showing the correct shape beside the scenario key is the only place it is read in time. 51-debug.md raised by one row for the same queued-OnFinal symptom, and by four lines pointing step 1 at run_scenarios.py --verbose: an agent that cannot get the raw output of a run from the runner starts the processes by hand, which section 9 of the runbook forbids and which cost 28 turns of an earlier run
